From: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
Subject: amdv: cap the LLDV VSVDB peak to the content's own mastering peak
 
In low-latency (player-led) Dolby Vision output the DV core takes its target
peak from the SINK'S VSVDB, not from the set_target_max_lum argument. That is
why the same title is graded correctly through VS10 HDR10 output - which uses
amdv_target_max[][] - and wrong in LLDV.
 
Consequence: a display advertising (or being told, via our vsvdb.maxlum
override) a 3000-nit peak, playing content mastered at ~100 nits, leaves the
DM with ~30x headroom. It then has essentially nothing to map, and every L2
trim scales to nothing - reported on-box as a "Positive Lift Offset Trim" test
pattern showing no lift at all in player-led DV while being correct in HDR10
output. Confirmed by dropping the injected peak to 100: the lift reappeared.
 
Fix, ported from avdvplus R9 (amdolby_vision.c:5668, its LL-only VSVDB block):
when low latency is engaged and the source is Dolby Vision, clamp the v2 VSVDB
maximum-luminance field handed to the control path down to the stream's own
source_max_pq (the ETSI base block at md_buf[66..67]). Never raises it, so a
title graded above the panel is unaffected.
 
This edits only new_m_dovi_setting.vsvdb_tbl - the in-memory copy passed to
multi_control_path. It deliberately does NOT touch force_vsvdb/vsvdb_data:
that path re-latches the EDID, which blanks the sink for seconds mid-playback
(the reason Kodi's aml_dv_apply_vsvdb carries a dv_cap liveness guard), and a
cap that tracks content cannot live there.
 
avdvplus also forces the VSVDB minimum luminance to 0 in this block. That is
not ported: the panels checked here already advertise min = 0, so it would be
an untestable no-op, and lifting a non-zero sink black is a separate decision.
 
Only v2 VSVDBs are handled (version field == 2), matching the only format our
Kodi-side injector understands; v0/v1 blocks are left alone.
 
vsvdb_changed IS DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED. An earlier revision of this patch
also recomputed the flag from the post-cap table, on the reasoning that the
caller rebuilds vsvdb_tbl from the pristine EDID every frame and compares it
against contents still holding last frame's CAPPED byte - so it reports "changed"
once per frame for the whole title. That reasoning is probably right, but the
recomputation is not worth what it costs:

- The BENEFIT is unprovable from GPL source. No GPL code anywhere clears
  vsvdb_changed; every assignment sets it to 1, so clearing it has always been
  the blob's job. Whether a per-frame flag actually harms the DM cannot be
  distinguished without the blob or an on-box trace.
- The COST is a demonstrable regression, and it is the very failure this patch
  is supposed to prevent. Deciding the flag from a function-local static cache
  means the cache is never invalidated when the DM's own VSVDB state is
  destroyed underneath it. The provable instance needs no assumption about the
  blob: on HDMI hotplug / AVR re-handshake / EDID re-read, amdv_control_path's
  else-branch zeroes vsvdb_tbl, sets vsvdb_len = 0 and vsvdb_changed = 1, and
  the DM is told "no VSVDB" - our block is skipped entirely because the length
  is now below 8, so the cache never sees the excursion. When the display comes
  back, the caller correctly sets vsvdb_changed = 1 again on the 0 -> 27 length
  change, and the cache then OVERWRITES it back to 0 because the returning table
  still equals the pre-unplug copy. The DM never re-learns the VSVDB and the peak
  reverts to the sink's advertised value for the rest of the title. The same hole
  exists at every site that resets the control path with invalid_m_dovi_setting
  (use_ll_flag / ll_rgb_desired toggle, src/dst format or resolution change,
  set_priority, pri_input, and the three "vd1/vd2 off, reset cp" paths).

A correct version would hoist the cache to file scope and invalidate it at each
of those resets. That is a larger change than the cap needs, for a benefit that
is still unmeasured, so the flag is left exactly as the caller set it.

One thing this does NOT establish, stated rather than assumed:

- The cap itself still keys on input[0]. With enable_multi_core1 and pri_input
  == 1 the DV stream is on input[1] and neither the cap nor the correction
  applies. Pre-existing narrowness, carried forward deliberately, not fixed here.
 
--- a/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
@@ -11157,6 +11157,65 @@
 	}
 	new_m_dovi_setting.input[IPCORE2_ID].valid = 1;
 
+	/* LLDV: the DM takes its target peak from the sink's VSVDB, so cap it to
+	 * the content's own mastering peak - otherwise low-peak titles get so
+	 * much headroom that the trims map to nothing. Ported from avdvplus R9.
+	 * In-memory table only; never the EDID (that re-latch blanks the sink).
+	 */
+	if (dolby_vision_ll_policy >= DOLBY_VISION_LL_YUV422 &&
+	    new_m_dovi_setting.vsvdb_len >= 8) {
+		static u16 last_logged_idx = 0xffff;
+		unsigned char *x = &new_m_dovi_setting.vsvdb_tbl[5];
+
+		/*
+		 * The cap needs this frame's RPU. A control-path run without a
+		 * VD1 vframe, or with a short/absent RPU, legitimately has none
+		 * (in_md is nulled at the top of this function and only refilled
+		 * for frames that arrived), so the cap is skipped for that frame
+		 * and the pristine table goes to the DM.
+		 *
+		 * vsvdb_changed is deliberately LEFT ALONE - see the patch header.
+		 */
+		if (new_m_dovi_setting.input[0].src_format == FORMAT_DOVI &&
+		    new_m_dovi_setting.input[0].in_md &&
+		    new_m_dovi_setting.input[0].in_md_size > 67) {
+			const unsigned char *md = (const unsigned char *)
+				new_m_dovi_setting.input[0].in_md;
+
+			/* v2 only: max_pq = 2055 + 65 * field (bits 7:3) */
+			if (((x[0] >> 5) & 0x07) == 2) {
+				u16 vsvdb_max = 2055 + 65 * (x[2] >> 3);
+				u16 src_max_pq = ((u16)md[66] << 8) | md[67];
+
+				if (src_max_pq && src_max_pq < vsvdb_max) {
+					u16 idx = 0;
+
+					/* round up so the cap never lands below
+					 * the content's own peak
+					 */
+					if (src_max_pq > 2055)
+						idx = (src_max_pq - 2055 + 64) / 65;
+					if (idx > 31)
+						idx = 31;
+					if ((x[2] >> 3) != idx) {
+						x[2] = (x[2] & 0x07) |
+						       ((idx & 0x1F) << 3);
+						/* the table is rebuilt from the
+						 * pristine EDID every frame, so
+						 * this fires every frame - log
+						 * only when the cap itself moves
+						 */
+						if ((debug_dolby & 1) &&
+						    idx != last_logged_idx)
+							pr_dv_dbg("LLDV: vsvdb peak capped to source %u pq (idx %u, was %u pq)\n",
+								  src_max_pq, idx, vsvdb_max);
+						last_logged_idx = idx;
+					}
+				}
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	if (debug_dolby & 0x400)
 		do_gettimeofday(&start);
 
